{% extends "pages/_example.jinja" %}
{% import "_api.jinja" as api %}
{% set example_slug = 'ci-observability' %}
{% set example_facts = [
  ('Level', 'Intermediate'),
  ('Needs', 'Python ' ~ python_floor ~ ' or later; nothing else, as the interpreter stands in for a test runner'),
  ('Install', 'Cuprum ' ~ cuprum_pypi ~ ' from PyPI; see Getting started'),
  ('Tested', cuprum_verified_on ~ ' against Cuprum ' ~ cuprum_pypi ~ ' on Linux, Python ' ~ python_floor ~ '; both runs repeated eight times, identical apart from the random stop-commands token'),
  ('Uses', 'sh.observe, ExecEvent, logging_hook, cuprum.sinks.GitHubActionsSink'),
] %}

{% block problem %}
<p>
  A CI job runs commands nobody is watching. When one fails, the log should
  say which, with its output grouped where a reader can find it, and the
  job's own telemetry should record every run without anyone parsing
  output.
</p>
<p>
  Cuprum reports a run through three channels at once here: structured
  lifecycle events to an {{ api.ref('observe') }} hook, start and exit
  records through the standard <code>logging</code> module with
  {{ api.ref('logging_hook') }}, and the echoed output framed for GitHub
  Actions by {{ api.ref('cuprum.sinks', 'GitHubActionsSink') }}.
</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block code %}
{% call ui.code_panel('ci_step.py', 'Complete', foot=[verified]) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import io
import logging
import sys

from cuprum import (
    Program,
    ProgramCatalogue,
    RunOutputOptions,
    logging_hook,
    scoped,
    sh,
)
from cuprum.sinks import GitHubActionsSink

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(PYTHON, name="ci")
python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)

events = []


def collect(event):
    events.append((event.phase, event.exit_code))


logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format="%(name)s %(levelname)s %(message)s")
workflow_log = io.StringIO()
sink = GitHubActionsSink(workflow_log, title="Unit tests", force=True)

with (
    scoped(catalogue=CATALOGUE),
    sh.observe(collect),
    logging_hook(logger=logging.getLogger("ci.commands")),
):
    result = python("-c", "print('42 passed')").run_sync(
        output=RunOutputOptions(echo=True, sink=sink),
    )

print("result:", result.ok, repr(result.stdout))
print("phases:", [phase for phase, _ in events])
print("workflow log:")
print(workflow_log.getvalue(), end="")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
{% endblock %}

{% block output %}
<div class="cu-pair">
  {% call ui.output('Expected output', 'stdout') %}
result: True '42 passed\n'
phases: ['plan', 'start', 'stdout', 'exit']
workflow log:
::group::Unit tests
::stop-commands::949162b00dd838c9
42 passed
::949162b00dd838c9::
::endgroup::
  {% endcall %}
  <div class="cu-prose">
    <p>
      The hook saw four {{ api.ref('ExecEvent') }} phases: the plan, the
      start, the output, and the exit. Each event also carries the program,
      the argument vector, the process ID, timings, and an
      <code>exec_id</code> that ties one run's events together.
    </p>
    <p>
      The workflow log is what GitHub Actions reads: the output inside a
      collapsible group titled <em>Unit tests</em>. The
      <code>::stop-commands::</code> pair around it, with a token that is
      random on every run, stops the child's own output being read as
      workflow commands. The <code>cuprum.start</code> and
      <code>cuprum.exit</code> records went to the <code>ci.commands</code>
      logger on stderr.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block failure %}
<div class="cu-prose">
  <p>
    The same step, failing. The sink adds one error annotation, titled after
    the group, which GitHub shows on the job summary.
  </p>
</div>
<div class="cu-pair mt-8">
{% call ui.code_panel('ci_step_fails.py', 'Failure path', foot=[verified], flat=true) %}
{% highlight 'python', 'cuprum-syntax' %}
import io
import sys

from cuprum import Program, ProgramCatalogue, RunOutputOptions, scoped, sh
from cuprum.sinks import GitHubActionsSink

PYTHON = Program(sys.executable)
CATALOGUE = ProgramCatalogue.from_programs(PYTHON, name="ci")
python = sh.make(PYTHON, catalogue=CATALOGUE)

FAIL = "import sys; print('1 failed, 41 passed'); sys.exit(1)"

workflow_log = io.StringIO()
sink = GitHubActionsSink(workflow_log, title="Unit tests", force=True)

with scoped(catalogue=CATALOGUE):
    result = python("-c", FAIL).run_sync(
        output=RunOutputOptions(echo=True, sink=sink),
    )

print("result:", result.ok, result.exit_code)
print(workflow_log.getvalue(), end="")
{% endhighlight %}
{% endcall %}
  {% call ui.output('Observed output', 'stdout') %}
result: False 1
::group::Unit tests
::stop-commands::6bcf93e9497b517e
1 failed, 41 passed
::6bcf93e9497b517e::
::endgroup::
::error title=Unit tests::exit_nonzero
  {% endcall %}
</div>
<div class="cu-prose mt-10">
  <p>
    The run's result is unchanged by the framing: <code>ok</code> is
    <code>False</code> and the exit code is 1, and capture still holds the
    output. The sink activates by itself when <code>GITHUB_ACTIONS</code> is
    <code>true</code>; <code>force=True</code> reproduces the framing
    elsewhere, as here.
  </p>
</div>
{% endblock %}

{% block policy %}
<p>
  The catalogue lists only this interpreter, and <code>scoped(catalogue=...)</code>
  makes it the block's allowlist. The observe hook and the logging hook are
  registrations local to the block: leaving it removes them, so a library
  that runs commands elsewhere in the process is not observed by accident.
</p>
<p>
  The logging hook records <code>argv</code> verbatim. Pass secrets through
  the environment or a file, never as arguments, and never turn untrusted
  arguments into metric labels.
</p>
{% endblock %}
